{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-68052",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}$ has two vertical asymptotes $x=1$ and $x=-5.$\n\nTo compute the one-sided limits as $x\\to (-5),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=-5$ from the right and from the left.\n\n| $x$ | $-5.1$ | $-5.01$ | $-5$ | $-4.99$ | $-4.9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-8.36$ | $-83.36$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $83.30$ | $8.30$ |\n\nAll values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to (-5)$ from the left and all values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to (-5)$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\\,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=+\\infty .\n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and II is false.\n\nFinally, since both left and right-sided limits at $x=-5$ are different, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196482/assets/41983c0d07e355a9bd12cf92af158c95f390a5785f4bbc1d08562c240f190623.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
